## 2. Linear Regression

Given a dataset:

$$
\mathcal D=\{(x_t,y_t)\}_{t=1}^{T}
$$

Assume an unknown linear relationship:

$$
y=W^*x
$$

The objective is to learn an estimate $W$ of $W^*$.

### Prediction and Residual

$$
\hat y_t=Wx_t
$$

$$
r_t=\hat y_t-y_t=Wx_t-y_t
$$

### Matrix Dimensions

| Variable | Dimension | Description |
|---|---|---|
| $x_t$ | $d\times 1$ | Input vector |
| $y_t$ | $d\times 1$ | Target vector |
| $\hat y_t$ | $d\times 1$ | Prediction |
| $W$ | $d\times d$ | Weight matrix |
| $r_t$ | $d\times 1$ | Residual |
| $L(W)$ | Scalar | Loss |
| $\nabla_W L$ | $d\times d$ | Gradient |
| $\Delta W$ | $d\times d$ | Parameter update |

### Loss Function

$$
L(W)=\frac{1}{2T}
\sum_{t=1}^{T}\|Wx_t-y_t\|_2^2
$$

Equivalently,

$$
L(W)=\frac{1}{2T}
\sum_{t=1}^{T}r_t^\top r_t
$$

- $1/T$: averages the loss over training samples.
- $1/2$: cancels the factor of 2 during differentiation.

